                                "markdown": "Path traversal risk exists when untrusted data reaches filesystem APIs (file reads/writes,\nfile metadata checks, resource loaders, or path-based utility calls).\n\nVulnerable example:\n\n```java\nimport java.nio.file.Files;\nimport java.nio.file.Path;\nimport java.nio.file.Paths;\n\npublic class FileService {\n    public byte[] read(String pathFromRequest) throws Exception {\n        // pathFromRequest is untrusted\n        Path p = Paths.get(pathFromRequest);\n\n        // VULNERABLE\n        return Files.readAllBytes(p);\n    }\n}\n```\n\nSafe example:\n\n```java\nimport java.nio.file.Files;\nimport java.nio.file.Path;\nimport java.nio.file.Paths;\n\npublic class FileService {\n    private static final Path BASE = Paths.get(\"/srv/app/files\").toAbsolutePath().normalize();\n\n    public byte[] read(String pathFromRequest) throws Exception {\n        if (pathFromRequest == null || !pathFromRequest.matches(\"[A-Za-z0-9._-]+\")) {\n            throw new IllegalArgumentException(\"Invalid file name\");\n        }\n\n        Path target = BASE.resolve(pathFromRequest).normalize();\n        if (!target.startsWith(BASE)) {\n            throw new SecurityException(\"Traversal attempt\");\n        }\n\n        // Safe usage: canonicalized, constrained path\n        return Files.readAllBytes(target);\n    }\n}\n```\n\nKey vulnerable patterns covered by this rule include `java.io.File*`, `java.nio.file.Files.*`,\nSpring resource/file utilities, and classpath/resource lookups with tainted path values.",
                                "text": "Path traversal risk exists when untrusted data reaches filesystem APIs (file reads/writes,\nfile metadata checks, resource loaders, or path-based utility calls).\n\nVulnerable example:\n\n```java\nimport java.nio.file.Files;\nimport java.nio.file.Path;\nimport java.nio.file.Paths;\n\npublic class FileService {\n    public byte[] read(String pathFromRequest) throws Exception {\n        // pathFromRequest is untrusted\n        Path p = Paths.get(pathFromRequest);\n\n        // VULNERABLE\n        return Files.readAllBytes(p);\n    }\n}\n```\n\nSafe example:\n\n```java\nimport java.nio.file.Files;\nimport java.nio.file.Path;\nimport java.nio.file.Paths;\n\npublic class FileService {\n    private static final Path BASE = Paths.get(\"/srv/app/files\").toAbsolutePath().normalize();\n\n    public byte[] read(String pathFromRequest) throws Exception {\n        if (pathFromRequest == null || !pathFromRequest.matches(\"[A-Za-z0-9._-]+\")) {\n            throw new IllegalArgumentException(\"Invalid file name\");\n        }\n\n        Path target = BASE.resolve(pathFromRequest).normalize();\n        if (!target.startsWith(BASE)) {\n            throw new SecurityException(\"Traversal attempt\");\n        }\n\n        // Safe usage: canonicalized, constrained path\n        return Files.readAllBytes(target);\n    }\n}\n```\n\nKey vulnerable patterns covered by this rule include `java.io.File*`, `java.nio.file.Files.*`,\nSpring resource/file utilities, and classpath/resource lookups with tainted path values."
